# UI Components

**⚠️ IMPORTANT: This repository should NOT use components from the `react-components` submodule.**
**This file documents component patterns and structure only, not for importing from submodules.**

Always check `submodules/react-components/` before creating new components.

## Component Structure

### Component File Organization

Component files should only contain the component itself and prop type definitions. All other logic (helper functions, data transformations, utilities, etc.) should be extracted into a separate helper file.

**File naming convention:**
- Component file: `MyComponent.tsx`
- Helper file: `MyComponent.helper.ts` or `helper.ts` (if in same directory)

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - All logic in component file
// MyComponent.tsx
type MyComponentProps {
  data: Data[];
}

function MyComponent({ data }: MyComponentProps) {
  // Complex data transformation logic
  const processedData = data.map(item => {
    // ... complex logic
  });
  
  // Helper function
  const calculateTotal = (items: Item[]) => {
    // ... calculation logic
  };
  
  // Component JSX
  return <div>...</div>;
}
```

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Component file only contains component and props
// MyComponent.tsx
import { processData, calculateTotal } from "./MyComponent.helper";

type MyComponentProps {
  data: Data[];
}

function MyComponent({ data }: MyComponentProps) {
  const processedData = processData(data);
  const total = calculateTotal(processedData);
  
  return <div>...</div>;
}
```

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Helper file contains all logic
// MyComponent.helper.ts
export function processData(data: Data[]): ProcessedData[] {
  return data.map(item => {
    // ... complex logic
  });
}

export function calculateTotal(items: Item[]): number {
  // ... calculation logic
}
```

**What belongs in helper files:**
- Data transformation functions
- Business logic calculations
- Utility functions specific to the component
- Complex data processing
- Validation logic
- Formatting functions

**What stays in component file:**
- Component function definition
- Prop type definitions
- React hooks (useState, useEffect, useCallback, etc.)
- Component-specific event handlers (can call helper functions)
- JSX rendering logic

### Defaults for Complex Types

Defaults for complex types (objects, arrays) must be defined outside the component function to prevent creating new references on every render, which causes unnecessary re-renders.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - New object/array created on every render
function MyComponent({ config }: MyComponentProps) {
  const defaultConfig = { theme: 'dark', size: 'medium' };
  const defaultOptions = ['Option 1', 'Option 2'];
  const mergedConfig = { ...defaultConfig, ...config };
  
  return <div>...</div>;
}
```

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Defaults defined outside component
const DEFAULT_CONFIG = { theme: 'dark', size: 'medium' };
const DEFAULT_OPTIONS = ['Option 1', 'Option 2'];

function MyComponent({ config }: MyComponentProps) {
  const mergedConfig = { ...DEFAULT_CONFIG, ...config };
  
  return <div>...</div>;
}
```

**When to define defaults outside:**
- Object defaults: `const DEFAULT_CONFIG = { ... }`
- Array defaults: `const DEFAULT_OPTIONS = [...]`
- Complex type defaults used in props merging, function parameters, or state initialization

**When defaults can stay inside:**
- Simple primitive defaults (strings, numbers, booleans) used directly
- Defaults that depend on props/state (use `useMemo` instead)

### Type Definitions for Complex Types

Complex types (objects, arrays with structure) should be defined as type definitions where needed or imported from existing type definitions. Avoid inline type definitions and `any` types.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Inline complex types
function MyComponent({ data }: { data: { id: string; name: string; config: { theme: string; size: number } }[] }) {
  const config = { theme: 'dark', size: 10 };
  return <div>...</div>;
}

// ❌ BAD - Using any
function processData(data: any[]) {
  return data.map(item => item.value);
}
```

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Type definitions defined or imported
type ItemConfig {
  theme: string;
  size: number;
}

type DataItem {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  config: ItemConfig;
}

type MyComponentProps {
  data: DataItem[];
}

function MyComponent({ data }: MyComponentProps) {
  const config: ItemConfig = { theme: 'dark', size: 10 };
  return <div>...</div>;
}

// ✅ GOOD - Imported from existing types
import { DataItem } from "@/util/data-fetch";
import { NodeInputProps } from "@/pkg/ui/helpers";

function processData(data: DataItem[]) {
  return data.map(item => item.value);
}
```

**Best practices:**
- Define types at the top of the file or in a separate types file
- Import types from existing type definitions when available
- Use descriptive names for complex types
- Avoid `any` - use `unknown` if type is truly unknown, then narrow it
- Reuse existing types rather than duplicating definitions

### Code Conciseness

Minimize code length while keeping it readable. Prefer concise one-liners for simple expressions, but maintain readability for complex logic.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Unnecessarily verbose
const config = useMemo(() => ({
    sortKey: sortState,
    onClickSort: handleSort,
    noEntriesText: 'No items found'
}), [sortState, handleSort]);

// ❌ BAD - Too verbose for simple object
const merged = useMemo(() => {
  return { ...defaultConfig, ...config };
}, [defaultConfig, config]);
```

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Concise one-liner when readable
const config = useMemo(() => ({ sortKey: sortState, onClickSort: handleSort, noEntriesText: 'No items found' }), [sortState, handleSort]);

// ✅ GOOD - Concise for simple merge
const merged = useMemo(() => ({ ...defaultConfig, ...config }), [defaultConfig, config]);

// ✅ GOOD - Multi-line when it improves readability
const complexConfig = useMemo(() => ({
  sortKey: sortState,
  onClickSort: handleSort,
  filters: activeFilters.map(f => ({ id: f.id, value: f.value })),
  pagination: { page: currentPage, pageSize: itemsPerPage }
}), [sortState, handleSort, activeFilters, currentPage, itemsPerPage]);
```

**Guidelines:**
- Use one-liners for simple object/array literals with 2-3 properties
- Use multi-line format when it improves readability (4+ properties, nested structures)
- Balance conciseness with readability - don't sacrifice clarity for brevity
- Complex logic should remain multi-line for maintainability

### Order in the components:

Use the following order when declaring new variables:

1. router (`const router = useRouter();`)
2. dispatch (`const dispatch = useDispatch();`)
3. selectors (values from the store)
4. states
5. queries and mutations
6. everything else (ex. references, constants.. )
7. useEffect
8. useCallback
9. functions

## Navigation

### Router vs href

Prefer router navigation (`router.push()`) over `href` for internal navigation. Router navigation is recognized as in-app navigation, while `href` triggers a full page reload and is treated as external navigation.

```typescript
import { useRouter } from "next/router";

const router = useRouter();

// ✅ Prefer router for internal navigation
<button onClick={() => router.push('/projects/123')}>
  View Project
</button>

// ❌ Avoid href for internal routes
<a href="/projects/123">View Project</a>
```

## Buttons

### Creating Buttons in This Repository

**⚠️ DO NOT import button components from `react-components` submodule.**

This repository uses different button patterns depending on the context:

#### 1. Ory Integration Buttons

For Ory flow components (authentication, settings), use the `Button` component from `@ory/themes`:

```typescript
import { Button } from "@ory/themes";

<Button
  name={attributes.name}
  onClick={handleClick}
  value={attributes.value || ""}
  disabled={attributes.disabled || isDisabled}
>
  Button Text
</Button>
```

**Usage:** Used in `pkg/ui/NodeInputButton.tsx`, `NodeInputSubmit.tsx`, `NodeAnchor.tsx`

#### 2. Native HTML Buttons with Tailwind Classes

For general-purpose buttons, use native `<button>` elements with Tailwind CSS classes:

```typescript
import { combineClassNames } from "@/submodules/javascript-functions/general";

// Simple button
<button 
  className="button"
  onClick={handleClick}
  disabled={isDisabled}
>
  Button Text
</button>

// Link-style button
<button 
  className={combineClassNames(
    "link",
    isDisabled && "disabled:opacity-50 disabled:cursor-not-allowed"
  )}
  onClick={handleClick}
  disabled={isDisabled}
>
  Back
</button>
```

**Available CSS classes:**
- `.button` - Standard button styling (see `styles/tailwind.css`)
- `.link` - Link-style button for navigation actions
- `.button-wrapper` - Container for button groups

**Best Practices:**
- Always wrap `onClick` handlers in `useCallback` for performance
- Use `combineClassNames` for conditional classes
- Follow disabled state patterns: `disabled={isDisabled}` with appropriate classes
- For Ory-specific flows, use `@ory/themes` Button component
- For general UI, use native buttons with Tailwind classes

**Example from codebase:**
```typescript
// pages/settings.tsx
<button 
 className="link disabled:opacity-50 disabled:cursor-not-allowed" 
 disabled={backButtonDisabled} 
 onClick={() => router.push("/cognition")}
>
  {t('back')}
</button>
```

## Creating New Components

When creating new components in this repository:

1. **DO NOT import from `react-components` submodule** - Create your own components instead
2. **Follow the component structure patterns** outlined above (helper files, type definitions, etc.)
3. **Use Tailwind CSS** for styling - check `styles/tailwind.css` for existing patterns
4. **Use `combineClassNames`** utility for conditional classes
5. **Wrap event handlers in `useCallback`** for performance
6. **Define constants outside components** to prevent unnecessary re-renders

### Component Examples in This Repository

- **Ory Flow Components:** `pkg/ui/NodeInput*.tsx` - Use `@ory/themes` Button
- **Custom Components:** `pkg/ui/Icons.tsx` - Custom SVG components
- **Page Components:** `pages/*.tsx` - Use native HTML elements with Tailwind classes

### When You Need a Component

1. Check if a similar pattern exists in `pkg/ui/` or `pages/`
2. Create a new component following the structure guidelines above
3. Use Tailwind CSS classes from `styles/tailwind.css` or create new ones
4. Document component props and usage in code comments
